  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Lecture core · Margin and scale",
      "body": "For labels $y_i\\in\\{-1,+1\\}$, a separating hyperplane can be rescaled without changing the boundary. SVM fixes the scale by requiring $y_i(w^\\top x_i+b)\\ge1$. The closest points then have geometric margin $1/\\lVert w\\rVert$, so minimizing $\\frac12\\lVert w\\rVert^2$ maximizes the margin.",
      "sourceType": "lecture",
      "math": {
        "latex": "\\min_{w,b}\\frac12\\lVert w\\rVert_2^2\\quad\\text{s.t.}\\quad y_i(w^\\top x_i+b)\\ge1",
        "explanation": "The constraints place positive examples on or beyond one side of the normalized boundary and negative examples on the other. The objective prefers the widest feasible separator.",
        "symbols": [
          {
            "latex": "w",
            "meaning": "normal vector of the separating hyperplane"
          },
          {
            "latex": "b",
            "meaning": "bias"
          },
          {
            "latex": "y_i",
            "meaning": "signed class label"
          }
        ],
        "name": "SVM margin normalization",
        "purpose": "Use it to remove the arbitrary scale of a separating hyperplane before measuring its geometric margin.",
        "sourceType": "lecture"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Textbook depth · Lagrangian and stationarity",
      "body": "Attach a multiplier $\\alpha_i\\ge0$ to each inequality. Differentiating the Lagrangian with respect to $w$ and $b$ gives $w=\\sum_i\\alpha_i y_i x_i$ and $\\sum_i\\alpha_i y_i=0$. Substituting back removes $w$ and exposes a quadratic program in the multipliers.",
      "sourceType": "textbook",
      "math": {
        "latex": "\\max_{\\alpha\\ge0}\\;\\sum_i\\alpha_i-\\frac12\\sum_{i,j}\\alpha_i\\alpha_jy_iy_jx_i^\\top x_j\\quad\\text{s.t.}\\quad\\sum_i\\alpha_iy_i=0",
        "explanation": "The equality constraint is the bias-stationarity condition. It says the total multiplier mass on positive and negative labels must balance.",
        "symbols": [
          {
            "latex": "\\alpha_i",
            "meaning": "constraint multiplier"
          },
          {
            "latex": "x_i^\\top x_j",
            "meaning": "pairwise input inner product"
          }
        ],
        "name": "Hard-margin SVM dual objective",
        "purpose": "Use it to eliminate the primal parameters and expose the dual coefficients that define the separator.",
        "sourceType": "textbook"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Textbook depth · KKT and support vectors",
      "body": "KKT combines primal feasibility, dual feasibility, stationarity, and complementary slackness. For the hard-margin problem, $\\alpha_i>0$ implies the point is active at the margin: $y_i(w^\\top x_i+b)=1$. These active points are support vectors; points strictly outside the margin have $\\alpha_i=0$.",
      "sourceType": "textbook",
      "math": {
        "latex": "\\alpha_i\\left[y_i(w^\\top x_i+b)-1\\right]=0",
        "explanation": "A non-zero multiplier can only occur when its inequality is tight. The support vectors determine the solution through the weighted expansion of $w$.",
        "symbols": [
          {
            "latex": "\\alpha_i>0",
            "meaning": "active constraint"
          },
          {
            "latex": "y_i(w^\\top x_i+b)=1",
            "meaning": "margin equality"
          }
        ],
        "name": "SVM KKT complementarity",
        "purpose": "Use it to identify which training points lie on the margin and therefore can carry nonzero dual weight.",
        "sourceType": "textbook"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Textbook depth · Kernel and soft-margin extension",
      "body": "Replace $x_i^\\top x_j$ with a positive-semidefinite kernel $k(x_i,x_j)$ to obtain a nonlinear boundary in the original input space. With slack variables, the box constraint becomes $0\\le\\alpha_i\\le C$; $C$ controls the tradeoff between margin width and violations.",
      "sourceType": "textbook",
      "math": {
        "latex": "f(x)=\\sum_i\\alpha_i y_i k(x_i,x)+b",
        "explanation": "The prediction depends on kernel similarities to support vectors. Kernel validity matters because the dual quadratic form should preserve the intended convex geometry.",
        "symbols": [
          {
            "latex": "k",
            "meaning": "kernel function"
          },
          {
            "latex": "C",
            "meaning": "soft-margin penalty"
          }
        ],
        "name": "Kernelized and soft-margin SVM",
        "purpose": "Use it to extend the margin model to nonlinear geometry or nonseparable data while tracking the new trade-off.",
        "sourceType": "textbook"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Assessment habit · Read the alpha pattern",
      "body": "If the labels are +1 and -1, the dual equality says the positive $\\alpha$ sum equals the negative $\\alpha$ sum. A candidate $\\alpha$ vector that violates this is infeasible even if every $\\alpha$ is non-negative. First check equality and bounds, then inspect which points have non-zero multipliers.",
      "sourceType": "ref"
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "title": "Feasibility check",
      "steps": [
        "Suppose positive-label multipliers are 2 and 1, while the negative-label multipliers are 1.5 and 1.5.",
        "All multipliers are non-negative and the positive and negative sums are both 3.",
        "Therefore the bias equality is satisfied. If C is finite, also check that every multiplier is at most C."
      ],
      "answer": "The dual equality is a balance condition, not a requirement that all $\\alpha$ values be equal.",
      "sourceType": "textbook"
    },
    {
      "title": "Identify support vectors",
      "steps": [
        "Compute or inspect each margin value $m_i = y_i(w^\\top x_i+b)$.",
        "$Points with m_i=1 can carry \\alpha_i>0; points with m_i>1 must have \\alpha_i=0 in the hard-margin case.$",
        "In a soft-margin problem, points inside the margin or misclassified can also be active, subject to the box constraint."
      ],
      "answer": "Support vectors are active constraints, not simply the closest points by visual distance before the hyperplane is normalized.",
      "sourceType": "textbook"
    }
  ],
  "criticalQuestions": [
    {
      "prompt": "Why does the dual contain pairwise inner products?",
      "focus": "kernel trick",
      "angle": "eliminate w using stationarity",
      "modelAnswer": "Stationarity writes w as a weighted sum of training vectors. Substituting this expansion into the norm produces $x_{i}$^T $x_{j}$ terms, which can be replaced by a valid kernel."
    },
    {
      "prompt": "What does complementary slackness tell you operationally?",
      "focus": "support vectors",
      "angle": "connect alpha to margin equality",
      "modelAnswer": "A non-zero $\\alpha$ means the corresponding margin constraint is tight. Those points remain in the prediction expansion; strictly separated points have zero $\\alpha$ in the hard-margin case."
    }
  ],
